      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 4 of 4 of a certificate (page 11 of 11). The fragment contains:\n\n1. A table with meter sizes and indicating ranges / verification scale intervals:\n   - Columns: Meter size [mm], Indicating range (maximum value) [m³], Indicating range (minimum value) [m³], Verification scale interval (maximum value) [m³]\n   - Rows:\n     - DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0,001\n     - DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0,001\n     - DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0,01\n     - DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0,01\n     - DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0,1\n     - DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0,1\n     - DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\n   Note: Decimal commas need conversion: 0,001 → 0.001, 0,01 → 0.01, 0,1 → 0.1\n\n2. Certificate metadata:\n   - OIML Member State: The Netherlands\n   - Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5\n   - Project number: 2496280\n   - Page 11 of 11\n   - Issuing authority: NMi (mentioned as \"NMi\" header)\n\n3. Production locations:\n   - ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\n   - ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n   - ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\n\n   These are production locations, not manufacturers per se. But the schema has manufacturers[]. Hmm. Should I include them as manufacturers? The schema says manufacturers[] with name and address_lines. These are production locations. The schema doesn't have a \"production_locations\" field. The instruction says \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" But these ARE present. I could put them under manufacturers since they are production locations of the manufacturer (ABB). Actually, they're likely the same legal entity family (ABB). Since the schema doesn't have production_locations, I could either omit or include them as manufacturers. Hmm.\n\n   Given the guidance to preserve content present, and production locations are a kind of manufacturing site info, I think including them under manufacturers is reasonable but might misrepresent. Alternatively, I could use matrix_tables to preserve it... that seems wrong too. Let me think.\n\n   The schema is layered: certificate, issuing_authority, applicants[], manufacturers[], certified_type, model_family, components[], characteristics, matrix_tables[], recommendation, test_reports[], revision_history[], footnotes[].\n\n   Production locations are facilities that manufacture the certified type — they're essentially manufacturing sites. I think putting them in manufacturers[] is the best fit given the schema. Each has a name and address lines. I'll include them as manufacturers with a note? No notes field. I'll just include them.\n\n   Actually, wait — this is a fragment of a larger certificate. Parts 1-3 probably contain the applicant/manufacturer info. This fragment adds production locations. Since I'm told to extract only what's in THIS fragment, I'll include the production locations. Including them as manufacturers seems the most sensible mapping since they're places where the water meter is produced. I'll do that.\n\n4. Revision history:\n   - Initial, 14 August 2020, First issue.\n   - 1, 30 September 2020, Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\n   - 2, 24 December 2020, Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\n   - 3, 02 August 2021, Additional evaluation for extending temperature to – 25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\n   - 4, 26 August 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\n   - 5, 23 November 2021, Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\n\n   revision is string: \"Initial\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\", \"4\",