IML Certificate\" — this looks like a page footer/header repeated on each page. I'll include issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}? Hmm, is \"NMi\" part of the certificate header? It appears right after the table and \"---\". Yes, it's likely a page header. I'll include issuing_authority.name = \"NMi\". But the fragment gives no address. Omit other fields.\n\n   Actually, wait — the production locations... the manufacturer is presumably ABB, but the fragment only says \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". The certified type is a water meter (R49). So category: \"Water meter\"? The schema example categories include \"Gas meter\" etc. The fragment mentions \"The water meter\". I could set certified_type.category: \"Water meter\". That's present in text. And type_designations — the meter sizes DN40...DN1650 are sizes, and model designations may be in earlier parts. In this fragment, no explicit type designation is given besides DN sizes. The DN sizes are size designations of models.\n\n   Should I include certified_type at all? The fragment mentions \"water meter\" category. I could include:\n   ```\n   \"certified_type\": {\"category\": \"Water meter\"}\n   ```\n   That's fine and accurate.\n\n6. Model family: The DN sizes are the model axis. The table has groups of DN sizes sharing characteristics. The models covered: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650. That's 28 sizes.\n\n   The table is essentially a config/model-level matrix: characteristics vary by meter size group. I should extract:\n   - matrix_tables[]: the full table preserved.\n   - characteristics.config_level or model_level: attribute × model matrix.\n\n   Given rows group multiple DN sizes with same values, using config_level with axis \"meter_size\" and conditions like \"DN40, DN50\" is compact. Or model_level with 28 models each. The schema example for R137 uses model_level with each model. Here, the grouping suggests config_level might be cleaner:\n\n   ```json\n   \"config_level\": [\n     {\n       \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_maximum\",\n       \"axis\": \"meter_size\",\n       \"values\": [\n         {\"condition\": \"DN40-DN50\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n         {\"condition\": \"DN65-DN125\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n         ...\n       ]\n     }\n   ]\n   ```\n\n   But also model_family.models should list each model. Rule 4 says populate model_family.models AND model_level. For R137 example, each row is a model. Here, each row contains multiple models. I think the cleanest:\n\n   - model_family.models: one entry per DN size (28 models), with attributes indicating group.\n   - characteristics.model_level: attribute × model with each model's value.\n\n   That's verbose but most machine-usable. 28 models × 3 attributes = 84 value entries. That's a lot of JSON but acceptable. Alternatively, config_level with grouped conditions captures the same info more compactly. The schema says model_level[] is \"attribute × model matrix\" and config_level[] is \"attribute × config-axis matrix\".\n\n   Since the varying dimension here is the meter size (which is a model variant — DN size is part of the model designation), I'll use model_level with per-model values, and also model_family.models with 28 entries. Hmm, but listing 28 models each as {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"} etc. plus model_level arrays with 28 values each × 3 attributes — this is verbose but complete and correct.\n\n   Alternatively, use config_level with grouped conditions: \"DN40, DN50\" etc. — 7 conditions × 3 attributes = 21 entries. This is more compact and preserves the grouping exactly as the table shows.\n\n   Which is better? The rule 4 example shows model_level per model. The models here ARE DN sizes. I think I should do both matrix_tables (preserving the raw table) and model_level with per-model entries. Actually, let me reconsider: the instruction in rule 4 for R137 says extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level. Following that pattern here: matrix_tables preserves the raw table; model_family.models lists all 28 DN sizes; model_level gives per-model values.\n\n   I'll do that. To keep it manageable, model_level entries:\n\n   - indicating_range_maximum (m³): DN40: 9999999, DN50: 9999999, DN65: 9999999, DN80: 9999999, DN100: 9999999, DN125: 9999999, DN150: 9999999, DN200: 9999999, DN250: 9999999, DN300: 9999999, DN350: 9999999, DN400: 9999999, DN450: 9999999, DN500: 9999999, DN600: 99999999, DN700: 99999999, DN750: 99999999, DN800: 99999999, DN900: 999999999, DN1000: 999999999, DN1050: 999999999, DN1100: 999999999, DN1200: 999999999, DN1350: 999999999, DN1400: 999999999, DN1500: 999999999, DN1600: 999999999, DN1650: 999999999\n\n   Wait — but \"Indicating range (maximum value)\" — is it a maximum display value? The value 9999999 m³ etc. Yes.\n\n   - indicating_range_minimum (m³): DN40: 99999, DN50: 99999, DN65: 999999, DN80: 999999, DN100: 999999, DN125: 999999, DN150: 999999, DN200: 9999999, DN250: 9999999, DN300: 9999999, DN350: 9999999, DN400: 9999999, DN450: 9999999, DN500: 9999999, DN600: 9999999, DN700: 99999999, DN750: 99999999, DN800: 99999999, DN900: 99999999, DN1000: 99999999, DN1050: 99999999, DN1100: 99999999, DN1200: 99999999, DN1350: 99999999, DN1400: 99999999, DN1500: 99999999, DN1600: 99999999, DN1650: 99999999\n\n   - verification_scale_interval_maximum (m³): DN40: 0.001, DN50: 0.001, DN65: 0.001, DN80: 0.001, DN100: 0.001, DN125: 0.001, DN150: 0.01, DN200: 0.01, DN250: 0.01, DN300: 0.01, DN350: 0.01, DN400: 0.01, DN450: 0.01, DN500: 0.01, DN600: 0.1, DN700: 0.1, DN750: 0.1, DN800: 0.1, DN900: 1, DN1000: 1, DN1050: 1, DN1100: 1, DN1200: 1, DN1350: 1, DN1400: 1, DN1500: 1, DN1600: 1, DN1650: 1\n\n   Attribute labels: canonical snake_case. \"Indicating range (maximum value)\" → indicating_range_maximum. \"Indicating range (minimum value)\" → indicating_range_minimum. \"Verification scale interval (maximum value)\" → verification_scale_interval_maximum. Meter size [mm] → the model dimension.\n\n   Hmm, wait. Actually \"Verification scale interval\" for water meters — the unit is m³. Yes, header says [m³].\n\n   Should the model_level include \"meter_size\" as an attribute too (in mm)? The DN number equals the nominal size in mm (DN40 → 40 mm). But I shouldn't fabricate the conversion... well, DN40 means nominal diameter 40 mm, that's standard. But the table's first column is \"Meter size [mm]\" with values \"DN40, DN50\". So the meter size value is \"DN40\" with implied unit mm. I'll keep model_id as \"DN40\" and not add a separate numeric attribute to avoid fabricating.\n\n7. matrix_tables:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"name\": \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\",\n     \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum\", \"indicating_range_minimum\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"],\n     \"column_units\": [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n     \"rows\": [\n       {\"meter_size\": [\"DN40\", \"DN50\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n       ...\n     ]\n   }\n   ```\n   Hmm, rows as objects per row; meter_size could be a string \"DN40, DN50\" or an array. The schema example uses scalar values. I'll use string \"DN40, DN50\" to keep it simple, or array. Arrays in rows should be fine for JSON. I'll use arrays to preserve structure. Actually, to be safe and consistent with \"object per row\", values could be strings/numbers. I'll use arrays for the size groups since multiple sizes share a row — that's the truthful representation. Let me use arrays.\n\n   Hmm, but for column_units, meter size unit is mm (header says [mm]). OK.\n\n8. Revision history: revision as string. \"Initial\" for the first. Dates ISO.\n\n9. Footnotes: none present.\n\n10. Recommendation: R49. The certificate number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" implies R49 (2013 edition). Should I include recommendation? The fragment shows the number which embeds R49. Rule: \"Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\" The recommendation is arguably present via the number. I think including recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\"} is justified — the certificate number encodes it. Accuracy classes: not in fragment. Hmm, but should I risk it? The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" clearly: R49, edition 2013, scheme A, NL1 (Netherlands issuer), 20.01 (cert serial). I'll include recommendation with id R49, edition 2013, scheme A. This seems sound.\n\n   Actually, is scheme A or B? R49/2013-A — the \"A\" in OIML-CS numbers indicates Scheme A. Yes. And certificate field scheme: \"A\".\n\n11. date_issued: as discussed, 2021-11-23 (revision 5 date). I'll include it. Hmm, let me reconsider once more. \"This revision replaces the previous version.\" The table: revision 5, 23 November 2021, \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\" The header says \"revision 5\". So the current certificate issue date is 23 November 2021. Including date_issued: \"2021-11-23\" is a sound inference. I'll include.\n\n12. oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\n13. Page total: 11.\n\n14. Certificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Should I include \"revision 5\" in the number? I'll keep number clean and capture revision via revision_history. Maybe add \"revision\": ... not in schema. Keep as is.\n\n15. Production locations as manufacturers: I'm somewhat torn. The schema has manufacturers[] — \"Name, address_lines[]\". The fragment explicitly labels them \"Production location\". Since the JSON schema has no production_locations field, mapping to manufacturers is the best fit (they are the sites where the meter is produced). The multi-part instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment. I'll include them under manufacturers[]. Alternatively, I could put them in matrix_tables... no. manufacturers[] it is.\n\nLet me also decide about issuing_authority: The \"NMi\" header. I'll include issuing_authority: {\"name\": \"NMi\"}. \n\nAlso certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". \n\nNow build the JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2496280\",\n    \"page_total\": 11,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2021-11-23\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom (UK)\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway\", \"Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Inc.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America (USA)\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN80\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN200\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN300\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN400\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN450\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN600\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN700\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN750\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN800\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN900\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1000\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN1050\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1200\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1350\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN1400\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1600\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1650\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_maximum\",\n        \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\",\n        \"values\": [ ... 28 entries ... ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_minimum\",\n        \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\",\n        \"values\": [ ... ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\",\n        \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\",\n        \"values\": [ ... ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size\",\n      \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum\", \"indicating_range_minimum\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"],\n      \"column_units\": [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n      \"rows\": [\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN40\", \"DN50\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN65\", \"DN80\", \"DN100\", \"DN125\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN150\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.01},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN200\", \"DN250\", \"DN300\", \"DN350\", \"DN400\", \"DN450\", \"DN500\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.01},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN600\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 99999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.1},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN700\", \"DN750\", \"DN800\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 99999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.1},\n        {\"meter_size\": [\"DN900\", \"DN1000\", \"DN1050\", \"DN1100\", \"DN1200\", \"DN1350\", \"DN1400\", \"DN1500\", \"DN1600\", \"DN1650\"], \"indicating_range_maximum\": 999999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 1}\n      ],\n      \"description\": null,\n      \"footnotes\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\"\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2020-08-14\", \"changes\": \"First issue.\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2020-09-30\", \"changes\": \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2020-12-24\", \"changes\": \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2021-08-02\", \"changes\": \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to – 25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"4\", \"date\": \"2021-08-26\", \"changes\": \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"},\n    {\"revision\": \"5\", \"date\": \"2021-11-23\", \"changes\": \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWait — should certified_type.type_designations include DN sizes? The type designations of the water meter itself (e.g., \"WaterMaster\" or similar) aren't in this fragment. The DN sizes are sizes, covered in model_family. I'll leave type_designations out of certified_type, or maybe include module_designation? Not present. Keep certified_type minimal with category.\n\nHmm, actually should I include certified_type at all? \"The water meter is produced at...\" — yes, the category \"Water meter\" is directly supported.\n\nNow the model_level values. Let me carefully list per model:\n\nindicating_range_maximum:\n- D